  "seven_q": {
    "Q0_posture": "This document takes a cautious, self-critical stance, acknowledging the limitations of the isomorphism while exploring its potential.",
    "Q1_identity": "An isomorphism record (ISO-009) that maps the principle of least action and Kolmogorov complexity to wisdom theology and divine simplicity.",
    "Q2_domain": "It lives at the intersection of physics, mathematics, and Christian theology, with philosophical implications.",
    "Q3_claim": "That the principle of least action and Kolmogorov complexity share a structural pattern with wisdom theology and divine simplicity, specifically in the form of global optimization with minimal specification.",
    "Q4_support": "The claim is supported by scriptural references (Proverbs, Matthew, James, Isaiah), mathematical formulations (action principle, Kolmogorov complexity), and theological doctrines (divine simplicity, wisdom theology).",
    "Q5_dependencies": "It depends on the validity of the principle of least action, Kolmogorov complexity, divine simplicity, and wisdom theology as coherent concepts.",
    "Q6_consequences": "If accepted, it suggests that the universe's compressibility is a signature of divine wisdom, and that theological simplicity and physical simplicity are structurally related.",
    "Q7_kill": "The isomorphism is killed if a fundamental physical law is found that is not derivable from an action principle, if the universe's Kolmogorov complexity is shown to be high, if divine simplicity is shown to be incoherent, or if the mapping is shown to be trivially true of any two rational frameworks."
  },
